 like based upon our  previous study and looking at this study  that it might be linked to greater  nutrient uptake  or possibly nutrient allocation through  mycorrhizal  foraging so now we're switching gears  totally to the last chapter  and here we're looking at landscape  scale depositions and movement across a  black carbon or i'm sorry a pyrogenic  carbon across  landscapes at the small scale and also  landscapes at the larger scale  so there's been quite a couple of  studies that have documented  preferential loss of pyrogenic carbon  soil pyrogenic carbon in the literature  and these have both come from  agricultural landscapes which is rumple  and uh and her colleagues in 2006  and from natural landscapes and this was  guggenberger and his colleagues in 2008  in addition to that there's been some  evidence of large-scale mechanistic  coupling between pyrogenic carbon and  total organic carbon on the on the  landscapes and so here  jaffe looked at global rivers at the at  the exports of pyrogenic carbon and  total carbon from global rivers and they  found a really  strong and significant correlation  between the two however there's really  no process based linkages between what  people have been seeing at the smaller  landscapes in agriculture and the nature  and also at the global landscapes with  rivers  so we wanted to do is we wanted to  quantify both the magnitude and the  pathways of terrestrial black carbon  loss and and translocations within the  landscape  as  a response from initial fire event  we also wanted to  see if we can take a stab at identifying  the mechanisms for these movements  within the landscape so hypotheses was  one that erosion would be the dominant  pathway for terrestrial black carbon  loss in the landscape and that pyrogenic  carbon would we would be preferentially  eroded from the landscapes  so we did this by using um  some of the  the paired watershed studies that have  been established within this department  over the past couple of years  in western kenya and if you remember  john retcha this is what he did for his  entire phd  so we have here is a pair of forested  watersheds a pair of watersheds that  were converted into agriculture  relatively recently so 2001  a pair of watersheds that were converted  in the medium term so in the early 90s  and a pair of watersheds that were  converted from the native forest  in the 1950s  so we were  fortunate but also unfortunate for the  landscape to have  been able to monitor and evaluate the  initial  deposition of black carbon in the  forested landscape so in this early  early 2012 the local population  communities began the process of  clearing one of our forested watersheds  and then they used the woods the trees  once they were cleared  to create charcoal so what we saw  is is really the initial  the initial deposition what most likely  occurred over the past 60 years in these  soils  and this wasn't uh what was classically  defined as a slash and burn where the  entire landscape is cut down and burnt  rather it's a chop and char so that the  trees are chopped down  piled together and then paralyzed  to create charcoal for export in very  specific locations located throughout  the landscape  so here are some of the land use maps  that we created for these watersheds  again  on this panel we have the young  agricultural watersheds  medium agricultural watersheds and old  agricultural watersheds and one of the  things that i would like to point out is  that the land use change  does occur and does change between  the different conversion ages so we get  a predominance of maize cultivation here  and then as we move over time we get  more and more change into uh perennial  crops i know that i didn't include the  key but this green area is maze and  these this  this green spotted area are is uh tea  and also some wood lots that we have  here  so we took these landscapes and we we  divided them divided them into slope  units  so these are this 