ogenic carbon  and total organic carbon  and in addition to that we took the rest  of the samples and measured pyrogenic  carbon and total organic carbon using  ftar and then used using the  partial least squares analysis we needed  a regression to make a predictive model  for black uh pyrogenic carbon and total  organic carbon for the rest of our  samples  so here are some of the results this is  uh with depth these are total organic  carbon and non-pyrogenic carbon and this  really follows what we would expect  in the surface we have really high  concentrations of these carbon species  and the concentrations decrease with  depth  now here we're looking at soil pyrogenic  carbon and then also the ratio of  pyrogenic carbon to total carbon  with  the total uh pyrogenic carbon what we're  finding is the same thing we saw before  actually this is up here  where we get our greatest concentrations  on the surface and we get a decrease  with depth  but unlike what we saw before  with the ratio of pyrogenic carbon to  total carbon we found an increase  in this ratio with depth so we're  getting enrichment in pyrogenic carbon  with depth in the soil  here we're also looking at soils except  we're looking at only surface soils and  now we're looking across the different  catchments so moving from the forest to  the old agricultural landscapes  and one of the things that we see here  is uh in the forest we're seeing really  kind of a random distribution  of total organic carbon and  non-pyrogenic carbon across the  landscape within the forested catchments  but with pyrogenic carbon as i mentioned  before we're really seeing very  site-specific increases in pyrogenic  carbon so these are the areas where the  charcoal was manufactured  as we move into the young agricultural  catchment with total organic carbon and  non-pyrogenic carbon we see an increase  in the concentrations and the total  amounts of these carbon species  along the topo sequence so we see a  decrease in the 